INT. CURLING CLUB - DAWN

Empty ice, grey-blue under the rafters. A frost-fogged scoreboard. COACH GREER WALLENBERG, 50s, thermos in hand, walks the sheet checking pebble, shoulders tight, a man with a final tomorrow.

A STRANGER stands at the rail, an elderly woman in a green scarf, hands tucked in her sleeves. She has slipped in out of the cold.

He sees her. He stops.

GREER
Rink's closed, ma'am.

STRANGER
So is the door you came through.

He glances back. It is. He turns again, wary, and she steps onto the ice in soft shoes, steady as if she has done it all her life.

GREER
Who let you in?

STRANGER
Nobody. I'm just early.

She stops beside a stone and nudges it with her toe. He bristles.

GREER
That's the championship stone.

STRANGER
It pulls left. You know it does.

He says nothing. She smiles.

STRANGER (CONT'D)
Margit Lindqvist. I won this club's first trophy in 1961.

GREER
Greer. I coach the rink that wants the next one.

MARGIT
Then listen, Greer. You grind that stone on the left side, and your girls will thank you tomorrow.

He stares at her, then at the stone. He crouches, runs a thumb along the running band. A burr. Small. Real.

GREER
How did you know?

MARGIT
It pulled left in 1961, too.

He looks up. She has already turned for the door, scarf trailing, leaving a line of small wet prints on the ice.
